id: pm-slack-m43u
title: "A Slack Retry-After header can park the process for weeks, because the delay we control is clamped and the delay a server controls is not"
description: "CodeQL flagged js/resource-exhaustion at index.ts line 1176 on the first scan this repository has ever had: a timer is created with a user-controlled duration. Traced to slackRetryDelayMs, which returns a server-supplied retryAfterMs verbatim while clamping its own exponential fallback to 8000 milliseconds. The bound is therefore applied to the value we compute and withheld from the value a remote supplies, which is exactly backwards. parseRetryAfterMs accepts any non-negative integer of seconds or any future HTTP-date, so a response carrying a Retry-After of 99999999 makes the process sleep for roughly eleven days on a single header. Slack real rate-limit windows are seconds to a couple of minutes, so a two minute ceiling never truncates a legitimate wait. This alert existed only because a CodeQL workflow was added to this repository today; before that nothing was looking, and a repository with no scanner is indistinguishable from one with no findings."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-31T12:01:18.293Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-31T12:01:19.653Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-31T12:01:19.639Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-31T12:01:19.639Z"
author: claude-orchestrator
resolution: "slackRetryDelayMs now applies SLACK_MAX_RETRY_DELAY_MS to a server-supplied Retry-After, so both the computed and the remote-supplied delay are bounded."
expected_result: "A rate-limited Slack response causes a short, bounded retry wait."
actual_result: "A Retry-After of 99999999 was returned verbatim and would have slept the process for about eleven days, while the exponential fallback it replaces was capped at 8 seconds."
files[2]{path,scope}:
  index.ts,project
  test/features.test.ts,project
close_reason: "Clamped the server-supplied delay to a two minute ceiling, verified revert-proof: reverting the clamp turns the retry-helper test red (182 pass, 1 fail) and restoring it returns 183 of 183."
body: ""
